---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { stats, devices } from '../lib/data';
import { paid } from '../lib/buy';
import Mark from '../components/Mark.astro';
const s = stats();
const nDevices = devices().length;
---

<BaseLayout title="About batteries.fyi" description="What batteries.fyi is for, where its numbers come from and how it is paid for. Nothing is ranked or recommended, and there are no display ads.">
  <article class="wrap pt-10 max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">About batteries.fyi</h1>
    <p class="lede measure mt-3">batteries.fyi helps you find the battery you need. Look one up by the name printed on it or by the device it goes in, and see its size, the other names it is sold under, what else fits and what to be careful about.</p>
    <div class="prose text-base mt-6 max-w-none">
      <p>The sizes, voltages and capacities come from battery makers' datasheets and from the IEC and ANSI standards. Nothing here is ranked or recommended.</p>

      <h2>What is here</h2>
      <p>The site covers {s.batteries} battery sizes, sold in {s.variants} combinations of size and kind, under {s.codes.toLocaleString('en-US')} other names. Each size has a drawing marked with its measurements, a 3D model at true scale that you can turn or download, every name it is sold under, and the sources behind every number. {nDevices} device pages say which battery a product takes, each quoting the device maker. Other pages compare sizes side by side in 3D, identify a battery from its measurements, explain the letters and numbers in a battery name, and work out run time, energy and battery packs.</p>

      <h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Every number has a source.</strong> Each value lists the datasheet, standard or catalog it came from and the date it was read. If no source publishes a value, the space is left blank rather than estimated.</li>
        <li><strong>Makers' own documents come first.</strong> The IEC 60086 and ANSI C18 standards for names and sizes, makers' datasheets for voltage and capacity, and parts distributors' listings and reference works only where no datasheet is available, labeled as such.</li>
        <li><strong>Capacity comes with its test conditions.</strong> How much a single-use battery delivers depends on how hard the device works it and the voltage at which the device stops, so every capacity shows the conditions it was rated at.</li>
        <li><strong>The data is checked every time the site is built.</strong> It is a folder of files, each checked against a fixed format whenever the site is built. A file that fails the check stops the build.</li>
        <li><strong>Every change is public.</strong> Every battery is one file in a <a href="https://github.com/centricle/batteries">public repository</a>. Each page links to that file's history, so any change to any value, and when it was made, can be checked.</li>
        <li><strong>The pictures come from the same numbers.</strong> 3D models, drawings and outlines are made from the same measurements the tables show, so they cannot disagree.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Free to use</h2>
      <p>The data, the 3D models and the code are published under the MIT license. You can <a href="/data">download them</a>, or read and correct them on <a href="https://github.com/centricle/batteries">GitHub</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="funding">How it is paid for</h2>
      <p>The information is free and stays free. Every page, the full dataset, the 3D models and the API are open to anyone, with nothing to sign up for.</p>
      <p>Battery and device pages link to a retailer’s search for the exact size, for the moment you need one. {paid ? 'Those links are Amazon affiliate links: a purchase can earn batteries.fyi a commission, at no cost to you.' : 'Today those links are plain searches that earn nothing. If they become affiliate links, this page and the links themselves will say so.'}</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>A link goes to a search for the size,</strong> never to a chosen brand or product.</li>
        <li><strong>Nothing depends on it.</strong> No entry, value, sort order or device listing changes with whether a link exists or pays.</li>
        <li><strong>No store link for loose lithium-ion batteries.</strong> Marketplace listings for 18650, 21700 and similar batteries often claim capacities no battery maker publishes, so those pages state the highest capacity in their sources instead.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>There are no display ads and no sponsored content.</p>

      <h2 id="mark">The logo</h2>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
        <Mark class="w-12 h-12 mt-1 shrink-0" />
        <p class="mt-0">The mark is two batteries side by side, head to tail, the way they sit in a remote. It is the picture most people have seen inside a battery door.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Who makes it</h2>
      <p>batteries.fyi is made by <a href="https://centricle.com">Centricle</a>. Its sister sites are <a href="https://cables.fyi">cables.fyi</a> and <a href="https://fasteners.fyi">fasteners.fyi</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
